#lang rhombus/static
// SPDX-License-Identifier: 0BSD

// A Theory-independent, type-checked reading of a `CoreExpr`.
//
// `module_block.rhm`'s runtime codegen and `elab.rhm`'s logical elaboration
// both walk the same `CoreExpr` tree and make the same type-propagation
// decisions (what does an `if`'s condition need, what does a numeral's
// expected type resolve to, ...), but do so with two separately written
// implementations, because `elab.rhm`'s path builds a kernel `Term` (which
// needs a live `Theory` for named-constant lookups and numeral-literal
// validation) while the runtime path runs at macro-expansion time, before
// any `Theory` for the user's module exists.
//
// A live `Theory` cannot be built at macro-expansion time to paper over
// that gap: `Theory.stamp` is minted fresh per bootstrap
// (`new_nat_type`/`new_basic_definition`/... each call `fresh_generation()`),
// so a `Theory` built once during compilation and a second one built again
// when the compiled module actually runs are not the same value and do not
// share a stamp lineage -- confirmed directly: `base_theory()` alone
// already fails a typed numeral literal with `check_term: undeclared type
// constructor Nat`, since `Nat` is only declared by the real pipeline's
// `install_native_nat`, itself a trust-sensitive, `Theory`-mutating,
// freshly-stamped kernel call. Reproducing that at expansion time would
// mean invoking real kernel definitional machinery from a macro
// transformer, paying its cost twice per module compile, and handing
// downstream code a `Term` whose apparent well-formedness rests on a
// `Theory` object with no relationship to the one the module actually
// runs against.
//
// `CheckedExpr` is the piece that actually is shareable: the type
// propagated through a `CoreIf`/`CoreNumeral`/`CoreRef` node depends only
// on a name's declared type (a plain lookup, not a Theory), not on the
// mechanism that eventually turns the checked tree into a kernel `Term` or
// into runtime syntax. `check_expr` is that shared, Theory-free pass.
// `CheckedInference` owns the one substitution shared by checked nodes and
// the still-unmigrated runtime fallback paths, so runtime lowering never
// forks inference state.
//
// Only one lowering exists so far: `module_block.rhm`'s
// `checked_to_runtime`, reading a `CheckedExpr` into runtime `Syntax`, no
// `Theory` involved. A `CheckedExpr -> Term` lowering for `elab.rhm` to
// consume -- the piece that would let the logical path share this same
// pass instead of its own independent walk -- does not exist yet; `elab.rhm`
// is untouched by this module.
//
// `CheckedOpaque` is the migration seam: a subexpression kind
// `check_expr` does not yet handle -- an overloaded `CoreCall`, an
// undeclared `CoreRef`, or a `CoreExpr` shape it does not cover at all
// -- is deferred to a caller-supplied `fallback`, which renders it
// however the un-migrated path already does and hands back its
// already-known type. A `CheckedOpaque` carries no further structure
// `check_expr` needs to see again.
//
// `fallback` is not the seam for every rejection, though: when
// `check_expr` already knows definitively that an expression is
// invalid with a simple, fixed-string diagnostic -- a numeral with no
// expected type at all, or a still-open expected type with no
// ambient numeric context; a `Pair`/list/set expression whose
// expected type has the wrong shape entirely; a set literal with a
// function-typed element -- reporting that through `fallback` would
// mean `fallback` needing the *exact* original implementation of a
// shape `check_expr` otherwise fully owns, not just the shapes it
// hasn't migrated yet. Those cases call the separate `~error`
// capability directly instead, with the exact original diagnostic
// message. A numeral's *third* invalid case -- a concrete but
// inadmissible expected type, such as `Boolean` -- is the one
// exception: its diagnostic names the offending type, which needs
// `type_to_surface_string`/the type constructor registry to format, a
// capability this Theory-free pass does not have; that one case
// stays on `fallback`.

import:
  rhombus/hol/htype open
  rhombus/hol/frontend/tyunify open
  rhombus/hol/frontend/surface_core open
  rhombus/hol/datatype/algebra open

export:
  CheckedInference
  make_checked_inference
  checked_resolve
  checked_unify
  CheckedExpr
  CheckedLocal
  CheckedConstant
  CheckedNumeral
  CheckedPair
  CheckedList
  CheckedString
  CheckedSet
  CheckedBoolOp
  CheckedEq
  CheckedApply
  CheckedIf
  CheckedOpaque
  check_expr
  checked_ty
  core_location

class CheckedExpr():
  nonfinal

// A reference already resolved to which of the two name spaces it came
// from: a local (lambda/let-bound) variable renders as a bare identifier;
// a named constant renders through the runtime's curry-adapting call
// machinery. Keeping this as two node shapes rather than one node plus a
// runtime re-lookup means a lowering never has to repeat the resolution
// `check_expr` already did.
class CheckedLocal(name :: Symbol, ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr
class CheckedConstant(name :: Symbol, ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

class CheckedNumeral(value :: NonnegInt, ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

class CheckedPair(first :: CheckedExpr, second :: CheckedExpr,
                  ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// `tail` is `#false` for a literal list (implicit `nil`), or a checked
// tail expression for `[a, ...b]`-style spread syntax.
class CheckedList(elements :: List.of(CheckedExpr), tail :: maybe(CheckedExpr),
                  ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// A string literal's own contents -- `checked_to_runtime` reuses the
// existing `runtime_string_expr` unicode-scalar-to-`text`-of-`Nat`-`cons`-
// chain encoding directly rather than reconstructing it, since it has no
// further type-propagation decision left to make once `check_expr` has
// pinned the result to `String`.
class CheckedString(value :: String, ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// A set literal renders as a predicate lambda, not a structural value
// constructor -- but its own soundness rejection (no executable
// equality over function values) already happened before a `CheckedSet`
// is ever constructed (see `check_expr`'s own `build`), so `ty` (the
// overall function-to-Boolean type) is all a lowering needs; the
// element type is recoverable via `dest_fun(ty)` on the rare occasion
// something else needs it, rather than duplicating it as its own field.
class CheckedSet(elements :: List.of(CheckedExpr), ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

class CheckedIf(cond :: CheckedExpr, then_branch :: CheckedExpr,
                else_branch :: CheckedExpr, ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// `conj`/`disj`/`imp` (arity 2) and `neg` (arity 1) render as Racket's
// own short-circuiting operators, not an ordinary named-constant call --
// `op` tags which rendering `checked_to_runtime` uses. Every operand is
// already `Boolean`, and so is the overall result: no separate `ty`
// field, unlike `CheckedSet`'s genuinely non-Boolean overall type.
class CheckedBoolOp(op :: Symbol, args :: List.of(CheckedExpr), at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// Equality renders as Racket's own `==`, not a named-constant call, the
// same reason `CheckedBoolOp` exists -- but is kept as its own node
// rather than folded into `CheckedBoolOp` because it needs its own
// bidirectional expected-type ordering (see `check_expr`'s own
// `CoreCall` case for `eq`), not a fixed Boolean expectation on every
// operand.
class CheckedEq(left :: CheckedExpr, right :: CheckedExpr, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// The general (non-overloaded, non-special-cased) application case:
// `head`'s own checked node together with its fully-checked argument
// list, in original argument order. By the time a `CheckedApply` is
// constructed every element of `args` is a genuine `CheckedExpr` --
// `check_expr`'s own two-pass scan/fill (see its `CoreCall` case)
// only ever hands this constructor its `out` list once every deferred
// position has been filled, and the field's own `List.of(CheckedExpr)`
// contract independently rejects a call that got this wrong rather
// than silently accepting a leftover placeholder.
class CheckedApply(head :: CheckedExpr, args :: List.of(CheckedExpr),
                   ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

// `opaque` is `fallback`'s own already-rendered runtime `Syntax`;
// `check_expr` never inspects it, only `ty` for further type propagation.
class CheckedOpaque(opaque :: Syntax, ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
  extends CheckedExpr

fun checked_ty(c :: CheckedExpr) :: HType:
  match c
  | CheckedLocal(_, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedConstant(_, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedNumeral(_, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedPair(_, _, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedList(_, _, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedString(_, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedSet(_, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedBoolOp(_, _, _): mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])
  | CheckedEq(_, _, _): mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])
  | CheckedApply(_, _, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedIf(_, _, _, ty, _): ty
  | CheckedOpaque(_, ty, _): ty
  | ~else: error(~who: #'checked_ty, "unknown CheckedExpr variant")

// Every `CoreExpr` variant's own `at` field. `check_expr`'s `fallback` seam
// needs this for a `CoreExpr` it does not itself destructure.
fun core_location(expr :: CoreExpr) :: Syntax:
  match expr
  | CoreRef(_, at): at
  | CoreCall(_, _, at): at
  | CoreNumeral(_, at): at
  | CoreIf(_, _, _, at): at
  | CoreForall(_, _, at): at
  | CoreExists(_, _, at): at
  | CoreFunctionExpr(_, _, _, at): at
  | CoreBlockExpr(_, at): at
  | CoreMatchExpr(_, _, at): at
  | CoreTypedExpr(_, _, at): at
  | CoreListExpr(_, _, at): at
  | CorePairExpr(_, _, at): at
  | CoreStringExpr(_, at): at
  | CoreSetExpr(_, at): at
  | ~else: error(~who: #'core_location, "unknown CoreExpr variant")

// A Theory-free type-checking pass over exactly `CoreRef`, `CoreNumeral`,
// `CoreTypedExpr`, `CorePairExpr`, `CoreListExpr`, `CoreStringExpr`,
// `CoreSetExpr`, `CoreIf`, a `CoreCall` to `conj`/`disj`/`imp`/`neg`/`eq`
// at their special-cased arity, and the general (non-overloaded)
// `CoreCall` application case -- the vertical slice proven end to end
// first. Any other `CoreExpr` shape (chiefly an overloaded call), and a
// `CoreRef` naming neither a local variable nor an entry `lookup`
// knows, is handed
// to `fallback` unchanged: this is the seam a later migration step
// widens, not a boundary `check_expr` itself needs to know the shape of.
//
// `needs_expected(expr)` mirrors `runtime_needs_expected` exactly: a
// pure (non-unifying) predicate deciding whether `expr` can only
// elaborate given an expected type handed in. `eq`'s bidirectional
// ordering and the general apply case's own deferred-argument scan
// both use it for the same reason -- unification is stateful and
// side-effecting, so this decision has to be made by inspection
// before committing to check an operand, not by speculatively trying
// one and rolling back on failure.
//
// `is_overloaded_name(name)` mirrors `overload_arity(...) != #false`
// exactly: the general apply case defers a `CoreRef`-headed call to
// `fallback` when its name is a genuine overload placeholder (not
// shadowed by a local, not already a registered constant), since
// overload resolution -- picking which underlying implementation an
// argument's own type selects -- is not something the general apply
// algorithm's "check the head's type, then its arguments" shape can
// do; it is still un-migrated, deliberately.
//
// One explicit inference capability owns the substitution shared by checking
// and every fallback that has not yet moved into this module. Keeping the box
// behind this small API prevents consumers from growing a parallel resolver
// or mutating substitution state directly.
//
// `lookup(name)` answers a named constant's declared (schematic) type, or
// `#false`; `check_expr` freshens it before unifying, the same way a
// polymorphic constant's every other use site does.
fun fresh_instance(ty :: HType) :: HType:
  let renaming:
    for values(out :~ Map = {}) (v in type_vars(ty)):
      out ++ {v: mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen(to_string(v))) :~ HType}
  type_subst(renaming, ty)

class CheckedInference(substitution :: Box)

fun make_checked_inference() :: CheckedInference:
  CheckedInference(Box({}))

fun checked_resolve(inference :: CheckedInference, ty :: HType) :: HType:
  deep_resolve(inference.substitution.value :~ Map, ty, [])

fun checked_unify(inference :: CheckedInference,
                  left :: HType, right :: HType) :: Boolean:
  match type_unify(
          checked_resolve(inference, left),
          checked_resolve(inference, right),
          inference.substitution.value :~ Map)
  | #false: #false
  | substitution:
      inference.substitution.value := substitution
      #true

fun check_expr(~inference: inference :: CheckedInference,
               ~lookup: lookup :: Function.of_arity(1),
               ~parse_type: parse_type :: Function.of_arity(1),
               ~vars: vars :: Map.of(Symbol, HType),
               ~fallback: fallback :: Function.of_arity(2),
               ~needs_expected: needs_expected :: Function.of_arity(1),
               ~is_overloaded_name: is_overloaded_name :: Function.of_arity(1),
               ~error: error :: Function.of_arity(2),
               expr :: CoreExpr,
               expected :: maybe(HType))
     :: CheckedExpr:
  fun resolve(ty :: HType) :: HType:
    checked_resolve(inference, ty)
  fun unify(left :: HType, right :: HType, at :: Syntax):
    unless checked_unify(inference, left, right)
    | error("expression has an unexpected type", at)
  fun check_sub(e :: CoreExpr, exp :: maybe(HType)) :: CheckedExpr:
    check_expr(
      ~inference: inference, ~lookup: lookup,
      ~parse_type: parse_type, ~vars: vars, ~fallback: fallback,
      ~needs_expected: needs_expected,
      ~is_overloaded_name: is_overloaded_name, ~error: error, e, exp)
  fun unify_with(ty :: HType, at :: Syntax):
    match expected
    | #false: #void
    | wanted: unify(ty, wanted :~ HType, at)
  match expr
  | CoreRef(name, at):
      match vars.get(name, #false)
      | found :: HType:
          unify_with(found, at)
          CheckedLocal(name, resolve(found), at)
      | #false:
          match lookup(name)
          | #false: fallback(expr, expected)
          | found :: HType:
              let fresh = fresh_instance(found)
              unify_with(fresh, at)
              CheckedConstant(name, resolve(fresh), at)
  | CoreNumeral(value, at):
      // Mirrors `runtime_numeral`'s admissibility cond exactly: a
      // numeral has a representation only at these three types. No
      // expected type at all, and a still-open metavariable (which
      // needs an ambient numeric context this call cannot supply),
      // both report their exact original fixed-string diagnostic
      // directly -- check_expr already knows definitively that both
      // are wrong, not merely a shape it cannot itself classify. A
      // concrete but inadmissible type (anything else -- `Boolean`,
      // say) defers to `fallback` instead: `runtime_numeral`'s own
      // diagnostic for that case names the offending type, which
      // needs `type_to_surface_string`/the type constructor registry
      // to format -- a capability this Theory-free pass does not have
      // and should not gain just for one message's sake.
      match expected
      | #false:
          error("a numeral needs an expected numeric type", at)
      | wanted:
          let resolved = resolve(wanted :~ HType)
          cond
          | resolved == mk_tyapp(#'Nat, [])
              || resolved == mk_tyapp(#'Integer, [])
              || resolved == mk_tyapp(#'Rational, []):
              CheckedNumeral(value, resolved, at)
          | resolved is_a TyVar:
              error(
                "a numeral needs a type ascription or an unambiguous numeric context",
                at)
          | ~else: fallback(expr, expected)
  | CoreTypedExpr(inner, type_stx, at):
      // The annotation is purely a type hint with no runtime
      // representation of its own: `inner`'s own checked node, unified
      // against the annotation and then (via `unify_with` below) against
      // whatever `expected` this `CoreTypedExpr` itself was checked
      // against, is the entire result. Matches both `elab.rhm`'s
      // `check_core(inner, annotated)` and the un-migrated runtime path's
      // `runtime_expr(state, env, inner, annotated)` -- neither wraps.
      let annotated = parse_type(type_stx)
      let checked_inner = check_sub(inner, annotated)
      unify_with(checked_ty(checked_inner), at)
      checked_inner
  | CorePairExpr(first, second, at):
      // Mirrors `elaborate_pair_effect`/`runtime_pair_expr` exactly: an
      // expected `Pair.of(...)` type destructures into its two component
      // types; a still-open metavariable gets two fresh ones, unified
      // back against it (so a sibling that later resolves this pair's
      // type also resolves the two components); anything else -- wrong
      // shape entirely -- reports the exact original diagnostic
      // directly.
      fun pair_component_types(wanted :: HType) :: maybe(List):
        match wanted
        | TyApp(name, [a, b]) when name == ty_prod: [a :~ HType, b :~ HType]
        | TyVar(_,):
            let ft = mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_pair_first"))
            let st = mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_pair_second"))
            unify(wanted, prod_ty(ft, st), at)
            [ft, st]
        | ~else: #false
      match expected
      | #false:
          let checked_first = check_sub(first, #false)
          let checked_second = check_sub(second, #false)
          CheckedPair(
            checked_first, checked_second,
            resolve(prod_ty(checked_ty(checked_first), checked_ty(checked_second))),
            at)
      | wanted:
          match pair_component_types(resolve(wanted :~ HType))
          | #false: error("a Pair expression needs a `Pair.of(...)` type", at)
          | [first_type, second_type]:
              let checked_first = check_sub(first, first_type)
              let checked_second = check_sub(second, second_type)
              CheckedPair(
                checked_first, checked_second,
                resolve(
                  prod_ty(checked_ty(checked_first), checked_ty(checked_second))),
                at)
  | CoreListExpr(elements, tail, at):
      // Mirrors `runtime_list_expr` exactly: with no expected type, the
      // element type is discovered by checking the first element (or the
      // tail, destructured) against `#false` first, then every element
      // and the tail is (re)checked against that discovered type --
      // matching the un-migrated path's own peek-then-render shape
      // rather than trying to check each element only once.
      // `list_element_type` mirrors `runtime_list_element_type`: a still-
      // open metavariable gets a fresh element type, unified back so a
      // sibling later resolving this list's type also resolves the
      // element type; any other wrong shape reports the exact original
      // diagnostic directly.
      fun list_ty(et :: HType) :: HType: mk_tyapp(#'List, [et])
      fun list_element_type(wanted :: HType) :: maybe(HType):
        match wanted
        | TyApp(#'List, [et]): et :~ HType
        | TyVar(_,):
            let et = mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_list_element"))
            unify(wanted, list_ty(et), at)
            et
        | ~else: #false
      fun build(element_type :: HType) :: CheckedExpr:
        let checked_elements:
          elements.map(fun (e :: CoreExpr): check_sub(e, element_type))
        let checked_tail:
          match tail
          | #false: #false
          | tail_expr:
              check_sub(tail_expr :~ CoreExpr, list_ty(element_type))
        CheckedList(
          checked_elements, checked_tail, resolve(list_ty(element_type)), at)
      match expected
      | #false:
          match elements
          | List.cons(first, _):
              let peek = check_sub(first :~ CoreExpr, #false)
              build(checked_ty(peek))
          | []:
              match tail
              | #false: build(mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_list_element")))
              | tail_expr:
                  let tail_peek = check_sub(tail_expr :~ CoreExpr, #false)
                  match list_element_type(resolve(checked_ty(tail_peek)))
                  | #false: error("a list expression needs a `List.of(...)` type", at)
                  | et: build(et)
      | wanted:
          match list_element_type(resolve(wanted :~ HType))
          | #false: error("a list expression needs a `List.of(...)` type", at)
          | et: build(et)
  | CoreStringExpr(value, at):
      // Mirrors `runtime_string_expr`/`elaborate_string_effect` exactly:
      // a string literal always has type `String`, unconditionally --
      // there is no destructuring decision to make from `expected` the
      // way a pair or list has, only `unify_with`'s usual check that a
      // caller's own `expected` (if any) agrees.
      let string_ty = mk_tyapp(#'String, [])
      unify_with(string_ty, at)
      CheckedString(value, string_ty, at)
  | CoreSetExpr(elements, at):
      // Mirrors `runtime_set_expr`/`runtime_set_element_type` exactly:
      // an expected function-to-Boolean type destructures into its
      // domain (the element type); a still-open metavariable gets a
      // fresh element type, unified back against a function-to-Boolean
      // shape; any other wrong shape reports the exact original
      // diagnostic directly. Unlike `CoreListExpr`, an empty
      // set with no expected type needs no peek -- there is no tail to
      // fall back on, just a fresh element type directly.
      fun set_element_type(wanted :: HType) :: maybe(HType):
        match wanted
        | TyVar(_,):
            let et = mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_set_element"))
            unify(wanted, mk_fun(et, mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])), at)
            et
        | ~else:
            if is_fun(wanted)
            | let (dom, rng) = dest_fun(wanted)
              (if rng == mk_tyapp(#'bool, []) | dom :~ HType | #false)
            | #false
      // Mirrors the original's ordering exactly: elements are checked
      // against `element_type` first (checking may itself refine a
      // still-open element type further), then the *post-check*
      // resolved type is what decides soundness -- a function-typed
      // element has no sound executable equality, reported directly
      // (check_expr already knows definitively that this is wrong, the
      // same `is_fun` rejection `runtime_set_expr` makes, not merely a
      // shape it cannot itself classify).
      fun build(element_type :: HType) :: CheckedExpr:
        let checked_elements:
          elements.map(fun (e :: CoreExpr): check_sub(e, element_type))
        let resolved_et = resolve(element_type)
        if is_fun(resolved_et)
        | error(
            "a set literal has no sound executable equality for function values",
            at)
        | CheckedSet(
            checked_elements, mk_fun(resolved_et, mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])), at)
      match expected
      | #false:
          match elements
          | List.cons(first, _):
              let peek = check_sub(first :~ CoreExpr, #false)
              build(checked_ty(peek))
          | []: build(mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_set_element")))
      | wanted:
          match set_element_type(resolve(wanted :~ HType))
          | #false: error("a set expression needs a function-to-Boolean type", at)
          | et: build(et)
  // Mirrors `runtime_expr`'s own special-cased rendering for `conj`/
  // `disj`/`imp`/`neg` exactly: each operand is `Boolean`, and so is the
  // whole call -- no function-type consultation, no overload
  // resolution, no deferred-argument scan the general apply case below
  // needs. Any other name, arity, or non-`CoreRef` function defers to
  // `fallback`, which is the general-apply/overload path this slice
  // does not yet migrate.
  // Mirrors `runtime_expr`'s own dedicated `eq` case exactly: whichever
  // operand `needs_expected` says can only elaborate given a type gets
  // deferred until the other, self-sufficient operand's own checked
  // type is available to hand it; if neither or both need one, check
  // both independently and unify their types directly. `eq` gets this
  // dedicated bidirectional ordering (unlike the general apply case's
  // uniform left-to-right-then-deferred-fill scan) because either
  // operand may need the other's type, not just a later one a domain
  // metavariable is waiting on.
  | CoreCall(CoreRef(name, _), args, at) when name == #'eq && args.length() == 2:
      let left_core :~ CoreExpr = args[0]
      let right_core :~ CoreExpr = args[1]
      let (checked_left, checked_right):
        cond
        | needs_expected(right_core) && !needs_expected(left_core):
            let left = check_sub(left_core, #false)
            values(left, check_sub(right_core, checked_ty(left)))
        | needs_expected(left_core) && !needs_expected(right_core):
            let right = check_sub(right_core, #false)
            values(check_sub(left_core, checked_ty(right)), right)
        | ~else:
            let left = check_sub(left_core, #false)
            let right = check_sub(right_core, #false)
            unify(checked_ty(left), checked_ty(right), at)
            values(left, right)
      unify_with(mk_tyapp(#'bool, []), at)
      CheckedEq(checked_left, checked_right, at)
  | CoreCall(CoreRef(name, _), args, at) when (name == #'conj
                                                 || name == #'disj
                                                 || name == #'imp)
                                                && args.length() == 2:
      let bool_ty = mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])
      let checked_args:
        args.map(fun (a :: CoreExpr): check_sub(a, bool_ty))
      unify_with(bool_ty, at)
      CheckedBoolOp(name, checked_args, at)
  | CoreCall(CoreRef(name, _), args, at) when name == #'neg && args.length() == 1:
      let bool_ty = mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])
      let checked_args:
        args.map(fun (a :: CoreExpr): check_sub(a, bool_ty))
      unify_with(bool_ty, at)
      CheckedBoolOp(name, checked_args, at)
  | CoreCall(CoreRef(name, _), args, at) when !vars.get(name, #false)
                                                && lookup(name) == #false
                                                && is_overloaded_name(name):
      fallback(expr, expected)
  | CoreCall(func, args, at):
      // Mirrors `runtime_expr`'s own general-apply `~else` branch /
      // `elab.rhm`'s `apply_arguments` exactly: an argument whose own
      // elaboration needs an expected type (a numeral, ...) and a
      // parameter whose domain is still an open metavariable are
      // useless to each other, so checking it is deferred until a
      // later argument's own unification has pinned the domain down.
      // "the applied expression is not a function" is reported via an
      // unconditional unify against a fresh function shape below, not
      // a direct error or a `fallback` retry -- see that step's own
      // comment for why.
      let checked_head = check_sub(func, #false)
      fun replace(xs :: List, index :: Int, value) :: List:
        for List (x in xs, k in List.iota(xs.length())):
          (if k == index | value | x)
      recur scan (rest :~ List = args,
                  index = 0,
                  current :~ HType = checked_ty(checked_head),
                  checked_args :~ List.of(maybe(CheckedExpr)) = [],
                  domains :~ List = [],
                  deferred :~ List = []):
        match rest
        | []:
            recur fill (pending :~ List = deferred,
                        out :~ List.of(maybe(CheckedExpr)) = checked_args):
              match pending
              | []:
                  // Every deferred index was visited exactly once by
                  // this recursion (it walks `deferred` to `[]`), so no
                  // `#false` placeholder survives to here -- made
                  // explicit (not left to `CheckedApply`'s own field
                  // check alone) by unwrapping every slot to a genuine
                  // `CheckedExpr` before it is ever passed on.
                  let final_args :~ List.of(CheckedExpr):
                    out.map(fun (v): v :~ CheckedExpr)
                  let result_ty = resolve(current)
                  unify_with(result_ty, at)
                  CheckedApply(checked_head, final_args, result_ty, at)
              | List.cons(i, more):
                  let idx :: Int = i :~ Int
                  let arg = args[idx] :~ CoreExpr
                  let domain = resolve(domains[idx] :~ HType)
                  let value = check_sub(arg, domain)
                  unify(domain, checked_ty(value), at)
                  fill(more, replace(out, idx, value))
        | List.cons(arg, more):
            let instantiated = resolve(current)
            // Unconditionally unify against a fresh function shape --
            // not gated behind "is `instantiated` already an open
            // metavariable", the way the runtime's own version reads --
            // so every case (already a concrete function type, an open
            // metavariable, or a genuinely wrong concrete type) goes
            // through the SAME mechanism. `unify`'s own failure reports
            // the generic diagnostic directly, without retrying
            // anything: no partial work from `checked_head` or an
            // earlier argument's own check ever needs to be redone via
            // `fallback`, and a still-polymorphic head is never
            // re-freshened a second time with a different instantiation
            // the way calling `fallback` here would risk.
            let domain = mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_domain"))
            let range = mk_tyvar(Symbol.gen("_range"))
            unify(instantiated, mk_fun(domain, range), at)
            let domain = resolve(domain)
            let range = resolve(range)
            cond
            | needs_expected(arg :~ CoreExpr) && domain is_a TyVar:
                scan(more, index + 1, range, checked_args ++ [#false],
                     domains ++ [domain], deferred ++ [index])
            | ~else:
                let value = check_sub(arg :~ CoreExpr, domain)
                unify(domain, checked_ty(value), at)
                scan(more, index + 1, range, checked_args ++ [value],
                     domains ++ [domain], deferred)
  | CoreIf(cond, then_expr, else_expr, at):
      let bool_ty = mk_tyapp(#'bool, [])
      let checked_cond = check_sub(cond, bool_ty)
      let checked_then = check_sub(then_expr, expected)
      let checked_else = check_sub(else_expr, checked_ty(checked_then))
      let result_ty = resolve(checked_ty(checked_else))
      unify_with(result_ty, at)
      CheckedIf(
        checked_cond, checked_then, checked_else, resolve(result_ty), at)
  | ~else: fallback(expr, expected)
